{% extends 'help/_layout.html.twig' %}
{% block help_title %}Connect Your Lingo or Stelo (CGM){% endblock %}

{# This page repeats the in-app wizard's steps (templates/connect_cgm/index.html.twig)
   in public prose, on purpose: the wizard is interactive/personalized (it mints the
   token and verifies live), while this page must render for anonymous visitors and
   ground the help assistant. Sharing partials would riddle both with conditionals. #}

{% block help_body %}
<figure class="figure d-block border rounded shadow-sm overflow-hidden mb-4 w-100">
    <img src="{{ asset('images/help/connect-cgm.png') }}" class="figure-img img-fluid mb-0 w-100"
         alt="The guided CGM setup with its live verification checklist">
</figure>
<p>If you wear an <strong>Abbott Lingo</strong> or <strong>Dexcom Stelo</strong> continuous glucose sensor,
{{ brand.name }} can receive its readings automatically and draw a blood-sugar response curve for every meal you
log. The one-time setup takes about 10 minutes, and {{ brand.name }} walks you through it: sign in and open the
<strong>guided setup</strong> at <a href="{{ path('app_connect_cgm') }}">Connect your CGM</a> — it creates
your access token for you, shows every value to copy, and confirms live when readings arrive.</p>

<h2 class="h5 fw-bold mt-4">What you'll need</h2>
<ul>
    <li>A Lingo or Stelo sensor that's already showing readings in the Apple <strong>Health</strong> app
        on your iPhone. (Check this first — if Health has no glucose, {{ brand.name }} can't receive any.)</li>
    <li>A small helper iPhone app, <strong>Health Auto Export – JSON+CSV</strong>, which reads Apple
        Health and sends the data on a schedule. Its automatic-sync feature needs the app's own small
        subscription (Basic is $2.99, or $6.99 for a year of Premium).</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="h5 fw-bold mt-4">The steps, in short</h2>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Install Health Auto Export</strong> from the App Store. When it asks for Health
        access, tapping <strong>Turn On All</strong> is easiest — or turn on only what you want
        {{ brand.name }} to see. <strong>Blood Glucose</strong> is the one this setup needs;
        <strong>Weight, Steps, Sleep, Heart Rate, and Blood Pressure</strong> are also put to good
        use in your charts and trends.</li>
    <li><strong>Create a {{ brand.name }} access token</strong> — the guided setup does this in one tap
        (or use <a href="{{ path('app_settings_api_tokens') }}">Settings → API tokens</a>). A token is a
        special password that lets the helper app — and only it — send data to your account.</li>
    <li><strong>Create a REST API automation</strong> in Health Auto Export pointing at
        <code>{{ public_base_url|trim('/', 'right') }}/api/health-metrics/health-export</code>, with an
        <code>Authorization: Bearer …</code> header carrying your token, <strong>JSON</strong> format,
        and <strong>Health Metrics</strong> as the data type — "Select All" is easiest ({{ brand.name }}
        ignores types it doesn't chart), or select only Blood Glucose plus whichever of weight,
        steps, sleep, heart rate, and blood pressure you want to share. The guided setup shows each
        value with a copy button.</li>
    <li><strong>Turn Summarize Data off.</strong> This one Export Setting matters most: when it's on,
        the app sends one daily summary number instead of your actual readings, and meal curves need
        the ~5-minute readings. A once-a-day sync cadence is plenty; a faster one just makes curves
        appear sooner.</li>
    <li><strong>Run the automation once by hand</strong>, then watch the guided setup's checklist turn
        green as your readings arrive — it checks automatically every few seconds.</li>
</ol>

<h2 class="h5 fw-bold mt-4">If readings don't show up</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Nothing arrives:</strong> confirm the Health app itself shows recent glucose; then re-check
        the automation's URL and the <code>Bearer</code> header (the word "Bearer", a space, then the token).</li>
    <li><strong>"Readings arrived but too few":</strong> that's the Summarize Data setting — turn it
        off and export again. The guided setup detects this case and tells you.</li>
</ul>

<p class="text-muted small">Glucose readings are health data — see the Health Data Privacy Notice linked on
the Health Metrics page for how {{ brand.name }} handles them.</p>
{% endblock %}

